To style different placeholders with different colors using CSS, you can use the `::placeholder` pseudo-element. This pseudo-element allows you to target and style the placeholder text within an input element. Here's an example:
```css
input[type='text']::placeholder {
  color: red;
}

input[type='email']::placeholder {
  color: blue;
}
```
In this example, we are targeting all `input` elements with the `type` attribute set to 'text' and 'email'. We then use the `::placeholder` pseudo-element to style the placeholder text within these input elements. The first rule sets the color of the placeholder text for all `input[type='text']` elements to red, while the second rule sets the color of the placeholder text for all `input[type='email']` elements to blue.

You can also use the `:focus` pseudo-element to style the placeholder text when the input element is focused. Here's an example:
```css
input[type='text']::placeholder:focus {
  color: green;
}
```
In this example, we are targeting all `input` elements with the `type` attribute set to 'text'. We then use the `::placeholder:focus` pseudo-element to style the placeholder text when the input element is focused. The color of the placeholder text will be green in this case.

It's important to note that the `::placeholder` pseudo-element only works on modern browsers, so you may need to use a fallback method for older browsers. One common fallback method is to use JavaScript to set the placeholder text color dynamically based on the input element's type attribute.

